Kubernetes Pod and Service Networks
Objective
Enable communication between application pods inside the same Kubernetes cluster using Services.
________________________________________
1. Software-Defined Networking (SDN)
Kubernetes uses Software Defined Networking to manage communication inside the cluster.
In OpenShift, the default SDN is:
•	OVN-Kubernetes
What OVN-Kubernetes Does
It creates a virtual network across all cluster nodes.
This allows:
•	Pod ↔ Pod communication
•	Pod ↔ Service communication
•	Containers inside pods to communicate
But:
❌ The SDN network cannot be accessed from outside the cluster directly.
________________________________________
Virtual Network Concept
All cluster nodes are connected to a single virtual network.
So pods behave like virtual machines on the same network.
They have:
•	IP addresses
•	Ports
•	Routing
•	Networking stack
________________________________________
2. Kubernetes Networking Capabilities
Kubernetes networking supports four communication types.
Communication Type	Description
Container → Container	Containers inside same pod
Pod → Pod	Pods communicate directly
Pod → Service	Pods access applications via services
External → Service	Outside traffic to cluster
________________________________________
3. Pod Networking
Each pod automatically receives an IP address.
Example:
Pod A → 10.8.0.1
Pod B → 10.8.0.2
Pods can communicate directly using these IPs.
Example:
Pod A → http://10.8.0.2
________________________________________
Containers Inside a Pod
All containers inside a pod share the same network stack.
They share:
•	IP address
•	MAC address
•	network namespace
Example:
Container A → localhost:8080
Container B → localhost:8080
Containers communicate via:
localhost
________________________________________
4. Flat Network Architecture
Kubernetes uses a flat network model.
Meaning:
•	All pods are in the same subnet
•	Pods can reach each other without NAT
Example:
Node 1 Pod → Node 2 Pod
Communication works directly.
________________________________________
Pod Network CIDR
Cluster uses a CIDR block like:
10.128.0.0/14
This range provides IP addresses for all pods.
________________________________________
5. Problem with Pod IP Addresses
Pods are ephemeral.
Meaning:
•	pods can be deleted
•	pods can be recreated
•	pods may move to another node
When this happens:
❌ Pod IP address changes
________________________________________
Example
Before failure
Frontend Pod → 10.8.0.1
Backend Pod → 10.8.0.2
After backend restart
Backend Pod → 10.8.0.4
Now:
Frontend → 10.8.0.2 ❌ invalid
The connection breaks.
________________________________________
6. Solution: Kubernetes Services
To fix this problem, Kubernetes uses Services.
A Service provides:
✔ Stable IP address
✔ Load balancing
✔ Service discovery
________________________________________
Service Concept
Instead of connecting to pod IP:
Frontend → Backend Service
Service forwards traffic to backend pods.
Example:
Service IP → 172.30.108.92
Service routes traffic to:
10.8.0.86
10.8.0.88
________________________________________
Service Advantages
Feature	Benefit
Stable IP	does not change
Load balancing	traffic distributed
Fault tolerance	failed pods removed
Scalability	new pods added automatically
________________________________________
7. Service Load Balancing
Example:
Frontend
   |
Backend Service
  /   |   \
Pod1 Pod2 Pod3
Service automatically distributes requests.
________________________________________
8. Services Use Labels and Selectors
Kubernetes services select pods using labels.
Example pod label:
app: myapp
Service selector:
selector:
  app: myapp
Pods with matching label become service endpoints.
________________________________________
Example
Pod labels:
app: myapp
Service selector:
selector:
  app: myapp
Matching pods become part of the service.
________________________________________
9. Creating a Service
Command:
oc expose deployment/<deployment-name>
Example:
oc expose deployment/myapp
This creates a service for the deployment.
________________________________________
Important Options
Selector
--selector
Defines which pods belong to the service.
________________________________________
Service Port
--port
Port where service listens.
________________________________________
Target Port
--target-port
Container port where traffic goes.
Example:
Service Port → 80
Container Port → 8080
________________________________________
Protocol
--protocol
Default:
TCP
________________________________________
Service Name
--name
Custom service name.
________________________________________
10. Viewing Services
Command:
oc get service
Example output:
NAME      TYPE        CLUSTER-IP      PORT
db-pod    ClusterIP   172.30.108.92   3306/TCP
________________________________________
Show Service Selector
Command:
oc get service db-pod -o wide
Output:
SELECTOR
app=db-pod
Pods must contain this label.
________________________________________
11. Viewing Service Endpoints
Command:
oc get endpoints
Example:
NAME     ENDPOINTS
db-pod   10.8.0.86:3306,10.8.0.88:3306
Meaning:
Service routes traffic to two pods.
________________________________________
12. Kubernetes DNS (Service Discovery)
Kubernetes provides internal DNS for services.
DNS server used:
•	CoreDNS
Every service receives a DNS name.
________________________________________
Service FQDN Format
SERVICE-NAME.PROJECT-NAME.svc.CLUSTER-DOMAIN
Example:
db-service.deploy-services.svc.cluster.local
________________________________________
Shorter Names
Inside same project:
db-service
Inside cluster:
db-service.project-name
________________________________________
Example resolv.conf inside pod
nameserver 172.30.0.10
search deploy-services.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local
This allows pods to resolve service names automatically.
________________________________________
13. Container Network Interface (CNI)
Networking in Kubernetes is implemented using CNI plugins.
CNI = Container Network Interface.
These plugins configure:
•	network interfaces
•	routing
•	IP allocation
________________________________________
Main CNI Plugins in OpenShift
Plugin	Status
OVN-Kubernetes	Default
OpenShift SDN	Older / deprecated
________________________________________
14. Open vSwitch
OVN-Kubernetes uses:
•	Open vSwitch
Installed on each node.
It manages:
•	routing
•	packet forwarding
•	network policies
________________________________________
15. Cluster Network Operator
OpenShift networking is managed by:
•	OpenShift Cluster Network Operator
It:
•	configures cluster networking
•	installs CNI plugins
•	manages network configuration
________________________________________
Check Network Operator
Command:
oc get -n openshift-network-operator deployment/network-operator
Example:
network-operator   1/1   Running
________________________________________
View Cluster Network Configuration
Command:
oc describe network.config/cluster
Example output:
Cluster Network CIDR: 10.128.0.0/14
Service Network CIDR: 172.30.0.0/16
Network Type: OVNKubernetes
________________________________________
Pod vs Service Network
Network	Purpose
Pod Network	communication between pods
Service Network	stable IP for services
________________________________________
Summary (Very Important for Exams)
Pods
•	each pod gets an IP
•	IP changes when pod restarts
•	containers share network stack
________________________________________
Services
•	stable IP
•	load balancing
•	service discovery
•	fault tolerance
________________________________________
DNS
Pods access services using:
service-name
instead of IP addresses.
